Z||000000||Pheagey||10-21-2001||09:21 PM||||Anyone know the price/cost/talent neede in replacing the headliner by themselves instead of a shop? thanks.<P>------------------<BR>Pheagey Grean<BR>"The time to talk is over. The time for diplomacy is over, the time for bluffing is over. The time to kick some f!@king as$ is now."<P>'88 coupe 'Hotwheels'<P>Http://www.LithiumEffects.com/car.html<P>Kills: '97 Civic Auto,<BR>Killed by: -||||reg||1
Z||000001||Monkeyman||10-21-2001||09:42 PM||||DO NOT WASTE YOUR MONEY BY LETTING A SHOP RECOVER YOUR HEADLINER!!!<P>I think I've made myself clear.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  It's a piece of cake.  Remove all of the interior trim including the sunvisors, overhead light console, coat hooks, and 3<sup>rd</sup> brake light if you have one.  If you have a sunroof, pull the plastic trim from around the opening.  Gently remove the headliner board.  It might stick slightly, especially if it's never been removed before.<P>Lay it down on a flat surface and gently pull the headliner material off of the board.  Take a broom (don't laugh at me) and gently sweep away the remaining headliner glue (it'll come off as dust after 15 years).  If there are cracks, etc, use some fiberglass mat to fix those, then cover the entire headliner board (front and back) with a good coating of resin only.  This will make it stronger and give you a smooth surface for the new glue.<P>Use some good spray on glue and recover.  You can try one of the kits (such as Heads Up! found in most Autozones, etc).<P>This is very easy.  On a difficulty scale of 1-10, this rates about a 2 (right behind checking your oil) unless your board has numerous cracks in which case it rates about a 4.<P>Here's another hint.  If you have a sunroof, <B>do not</B> cut out the sunroof area until the board is back in the car.  Do this last.  Once it's back in the car, leave an inch of material, then use a credit card to tuck that into the groove.<P>Oops.  Forgot the price.  Less than $30 even if you use one of the kits.  I did mine for about $12 but I'm going to cover a fiberglass board next month with vinyl so it'll run me around $125 (including the great deal on the fiberglass board). <P>If you have any further questions, email me.<p>[This message has been edited by Monkeyman (edited 10-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||85ZainoGT||10-21-2001||10:38 PM||||monkeyman, how far are you from plainfield?  How long does it take to do the whole replacement process?  me and another Indiana guy are going to plan a get together soon, if it within a couple weeks, we can call it my B-day bash!! but anyways, if you dont have the busiest schedule maybe you could help me?  IM me sometime if you have AIM or AOL.... automaticj5<P>------------------<BR>Paul Ventimiglia<BR>1985 White/Silver GT <BR>2.8L 4 Speed LOADED  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sigpic2529_1.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by 85ZainoGT (edited 10-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||10-22-2001||07:15 PM||||I'm probably a good 2 hours north of you.  I usually work at least a half day on Saturdays and I'm on call 24/7 (I manage a C store) so going too far out of town is difficult.  If you wanted to make the trip up here, I'd be happy to give you a hand.  My front porch (I use it for a workshop  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ) has seen many Fiero projects.<P>It'll take a good half day to do it right.  It's easier with 2 people, but a third might get in the way when you're trying to remove the interior.  If you were planning on repainting your interior, this would be a good time as you have to remove most of it to do the job right (IMO).  It's not a difficult job, just a little time consuming.  If you want a good, step-by-step rundown of what it takes, email me and I'll help if I can.||||reg||1
